WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.318
Twice now, a target has been out of reach

2
00:00:06.318 --> 00:00:08.841
and we stopped at no solution.

3
00:00:08.841 --> 00:00:11.445
Today we ask a better question

4
00:00:11.445 --> 00:00:14.700
what is the closest place we can reach?

5
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:18.226
Our robot's moves both point along one

6
00:00:18.226 --> 00:00:18.796
three.

7
00:00:18.878 --> 00:00:21.484
So every place it can reach lies

8
00:00:21.565 --> 00:00:24.090
on one line through the origin.

9
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.533
A customer waits at seven, five

10
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:28.887
off the line.

11
00:00:28.887 --> 00:00:31.690
Where should the robot stop

12
00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:34.397
to be as close as possible?

13
00:00:34.397 --> 00:00:36.040
Pause and guess.

14
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:44.335
Name the stopping point p: some amount

15
00:00:44.335 --> 00:00:45.906
x hat, of a.

16
00:00:45.906 --> 00:00:50.500
The gap e is what is left, b minus p.

17
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.798
Slide the stop along the

18
00:00:52.879 --> 00:00:54.745
line and watch the gap.

19
00:00:54.827 --> 00:00:57.099
It is shortest when it meets

20
00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.290
the line at a right angle.

21
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.540
Right angle means a dot e is zero.

22
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.751
Write e as b minus x hat a

23
00:01:06.751 --> 00:01:09.160
and solve for x hat.

24
00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.749
x hat is a dot b over a dot a

25
00:01:12.749 --> 00:01:17.067
seven plus fifteen, over one plus nine.

26
00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:19.550
That is eleven fifths.

27
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:24.129
So p is two point two, six point six

28
00:01:24.129 --> 00:01:27.900
and the gap is four point eight

29
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.300
minus one point six.

30
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.367
Check the right angle

31
00:01:32.445 --> 00:01:34.552
four point eight minus four

32
00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:36.190
point eight is zero.

33
00:01:36.268 --> 00:01:38.219
The robot ends about five

34
00:01:38.297 --> 00:01:40.170
units from the customer.

35
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:42.827
Is it really the closest?

36
00:01:42.827 --> 00:01:44.655
Test two other stops.

37
00:01:44.738 --> 00:01:47.147
Stopping at a itself leaves a

38
00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.390
squared distance of forty.

39
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.001
Three moves, at three, nine

40
00:01:52.001 --> 00:01:53.454
leave thirty two.

41
00:01:53.454 --> 00:01:56.360
And p leaves twenty five point six

42
00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.540
the smallest of the three.

43
00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:00.783
Look at the pattern.

44
00:02:00.877 --> 00:02:03.716
Forty is twenty five point six

45
00:02:03.811 --> 00:02:06.176
plus fourteen point four.

46
00:02:06.271 --> 00:02:09.204
Thirty two is twenty five point

47
00:02:09.299 --> 00:02:11.570
six plus six point four.

48
00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.086
Each triangle has a right angle at p.

49
00:02:15.086 --> 00:02:16.797
The gap is one leg

50
00:02:16.797 --> 00:02:20.049
the walk along the line is the other

51
00:02:20.049 --> 00:02:21.760
that is Pythagoras.

52
00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.130
So any other stop adds a positive piece.

53
00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:30.350
Part two turns this picture into a proof.

54
00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.438
Now look at the formula again.

55
00:02:34.438 --> 00:02:35.373
p is a

56
00:02:35.373 --> 00:02:40.050
times a transpose b over a transpose a.

57
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.976
Move b to the end.

58
00:02:42.063 --> 00:02:44.164
The matrix in front of b

59
00:02:44.251 --> 00:02:47.840
does the projecting, for every b at once.

60
00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:53.235
For the robot it is one tenth of one, three

61
00:02:53.235 --> 00:02:54.726
three, nine.

62
00:02:54.726 --> 00:02:57.937
It is a column times a row

63
00:02:57.937 --> 00:03:00.230
so its rank is one.

64
00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.094
A customer already on the line, at three

65
00:03:04.094 --> 00:03:07.009
nine, is their own closest point.

66
00:03:07.094 --> 00:03:09.837
A customer twice as far out gets

67
00:03:09.923 --> 00:03:11.980
a stop twice as far out.

68
00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:15.866
And projecting twice changes nothing

69
00:03:15.866 --> 00:03:19.114
because p is already on the line.

70
00:03:19.114 --> 00:03:20.930
So P squared is P.

71
00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.749
I minus P keeps the other part, the gap.

72
00:03:24.836 --> 00:03:27.871
Every gap lies on the perpendicular

73
00:03:27.958 --> 00:03:30.560
line through three, minus one.

74
00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.848
The robot has a second move, two, six

75
00:03:33.848 --> 00:03:35.131
on the same line.

76
00:03:35.202 --> 00:03:37.553
Would describing the line with it

77
00:03:37.625 --> 00:03:39.620
give a different projection?

78
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.449
Use the second move instead.

79
00:03:42.529 --> 00:03:44.377
The top of the fraction

80
00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:46.224
gets a factor of four

81
00:03:46.224 --> 00:03:48.152
and so does the bottom.

82
00:03:48.152 --> 00:03:50.160
They cancel: the same P.

83
00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:53.234
What changes is the amount.

84
00:03:53.234 --> 00:03:55.454
Eleven fifths of one

85
00:03:55.454 --> 00:03:59.590
three becomes eleven tenths of two, six.

86
00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.993
Both land on the same point, two point two

87
00:04:03.993 --> 00:04:05.407
six point six.

88
00:04:05.407 --> 00:04:06.916
p is the point.

89
00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.403
x hat is only an amount of whichever

90
00:04:10.498 --> 00:04:12.100
vector you chose.

91
00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:14.866
So, to project onto a line

92
00:04:14.947 --> 00:04:17.294
drop the gap so that it meets

93
00:04:17.375 --> 00:04:19.480
the line at a right angle.

94
00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:23.930
The amount is a dot b over a dot a

95
00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:28.030
and the point is that amount of a.

96
00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:32.372
The matrix a a transpose over a transpose

97
00:04:32.469 --> 00:04:34.611
a does it for every b

98
00:04:34.611 --> 00:04:38.700
and projecting twice is the same as once.

99
00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:41.928
Try one yourself: project five

100
00:04:41.928 --> 00:04:44.063
five onto the same line

101
00:04:44.148 --> 00:04:46.283
and check that the gap is

102
00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:48.760
perpendicular to one, three.

103
00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.698
In part two the drone has a whole

104
00:04:51.777 --> 00:04:53.424
plane to choose from

105
00:04:53.502 --> 00:04:55.385
and one division becomes

106
00:04:55.463 --> 00:04:57.110
the normal equations.
